<script>
	import Callout from '$lib/components/blog/Callout.svelte';
	import blogTest from '$lib/assets/blog-test.png';
</script>

This post is written in **Markdown** and rendered into the shared blog layout.
The title, description, and byline above all come from the frontmatter at the top
of this file — everything below is the body.

## Markdown just works

You get the usual formatting: **bold**, _italic_, [links](https://austinsteinhart.com),
inline `code`, and lists:

- Write prose without hand-writing HTML
- Every post stays on the same grid
- Add a post by dropping a folder in `src/routes/blog/`

## Drop in Svelte components

Because this is mdsvex, you can import and use real Svelte components in the middle
of the Markdown — but only in the posts that need them.

<Callout type="warn">
	This box is a Svelte component (<code>Callout.svelte</code>) embedded directly in the
	Markdown. Interactive charts, D3, or scrollytelling drop in exactly the same way.
</Callout>

### Images

Reference an imported asset with a normal HTML tag inside the post:

<img src={blogTest} alt="Example image" />

For images you keep in `static/`, plain Markdown works too: `![alt](/my-image.png)`.

## Code blocks

```js
function greet(name) {
	return `hello, ${name}`;
}
```

That's it — write Markdown, and reach for a component only when you need one.
